= Birth of a Party

The Left of Canadian politics has historically been fraught with division and isolation. The Progressive Party failed miserably to mount a united front against the two old-line parties and party in-fighting destroyed any respect it once held. Out of its ashes sprung the United Farmer's Parties but then still, they remain divided along Provincial lines, unable to contest the Prime Ministership. 

The Political Labour movement is doing even worse. The TLC has been noncommittal at best to the Labour and Socialist parties. What few Leftist parties exist are divided geographically and ideologically, from the Marxists of the Socialist Party of Canada to the moderates of the Independent Labour Party in Manitoba. 

Now however, a collection of Labour, Farmer, and Socialist organizations are set to meet in Calgary to discuss the formation of a new Party devoted to the cause of Socialism and Social Democracy

Delegates from the UFC(SS) and our ILP members have been invited to the meeting. Now we must establish what exactly we will ask for...

- @ccf_name: What name will we advocate for the new party?
- @ccf_policy: Do we seek a purely Socialist Federal Party or a Big-tent Reform Coalition?

